WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.549 --> 00:00:14.309
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here. Today we’ve got
a self-­aware, fourth­-wall-­breaking spectacular

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:17.029
for you. That’s right, we’re talking about Deadpool.

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:22.080
Deadpool is experiencing unprecedented popularity
in mainstream media. The film is the highest

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:26.269
grossing R­-rated movie ever, and the
biggest opening for Ryan Reynolds, but I guess

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:30.829
that part isn’t a surprise. How has this
film achieved such success despite shirking

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:32.720
the traditional superhero formula?

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.320
"Bob? I haven't seen you since-"

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.340
"Jacksonville."

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.060
Fighting for the greater good? Nope. Selfless?

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.560
Not really. Fun for the whole family? Uh...

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.020
"I'm touching myself tonight."

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:42.640
No.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.140
For a Marvel character who is an overt ripoff
of a DC competitor that constantly makes fun

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.379
of the medium that he inhabits, the merc with
a mouth is doing pretty well for himself.

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:55.539
Deadpool’s popularity represents a trend
in media that may be gaining steam lately,

00:00:55.539 --> 00:01:00.559
but is otherwise quite old. And it all has
to do with a 400 year old book, sort of.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.320
I’m an arrogant asshole and welcome to this
Wisecrack Edition on The Philosophy of Deadpool.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.400
Part 1: Achieving Fuck It

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.800
There are a few defining features that set
Deadpool apart from the rest of the Marvel

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.060
universe. He’s an inveterate wiseass,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.880
he knows he’s a superhero and is, as a result,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.040
constantly breaking the fourth wall and commenting
on comic book tropes and culture.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.980
Deadpool takes nothing seriously.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.700
Even the ad campaign for the film was imagined in an entirely Deadpoolian fashion.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.520
The film’s Valentine’s Day ads
were simultaneously a great way to troll soon­-to-­be

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:52.360
furious partners and a set­up for a self­-referential
joke. They even created a Tinder profile.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.780
Perhaps the best way to describe Deadpool
is as a postmodern work. Let me explain.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.000
Postmodernism is a movement in the arts and
philosophy that typically rejects the kind

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.920
of grand narratives used to make sense of
the world in the good old days. For instance,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.030
the battle of good against evil, democracy
against communism, the inherent promise of

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:14.620
technological progress, science or divine
salvation, the very idea of “the good old days”.­

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.390
You get the idea. Postmodern
works are quick to thumb their nose at tradition

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.980
and have a tendency to embrace being “meta”
and self­-reference.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.580
Deadpool is hyper-­aware of himself and his
place in the superhero genre.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:37.020
The film is full of Ryan Reynolds jokes, like the reference
to the cinematic abomination “The Green Lantern",

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:41.560
his face on People Magazine’s “Sexiest
Man Alive” or the face of his vastly more

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.380
appreciated counterpart ­ also on People
Magazine.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:49.200
If the grand narrative that permeates superhero
films is the triumph of good over evil, Deadpool,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.780
in good postmodern fashion, says “fuck it
­ let’s go bowling,” I mean, killing.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:58.430
Wrong movie - same idea. There is no grand
battle of good over evil, just a guy out there

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.900
killing a bunch of people for revenge and
love.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:08.400
This tension, between the old world and postmodernism,
is played out in a couple different ways in

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.420
the film. Colossus is constantly asking Deadpool
to cut out the shenanigans and join the X­Men,

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.240
as if to rope him into the role of a good
superhero, family friendly, righteous, and so on.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.120
Likewise, the Dopinder subplot subverts
classic narratives of courtly love. Win the

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.830
heart of your beloved through chivalry and
compassion? Nah. It’s more effective to

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.810
to just kidnap her current lover and stuff
him in the trunk. Even Blind Al is essentially

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.000
a deranged version of a classic narrative.
Deformed protagonist who takes refuge with

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.800
a benevolent blind benefactor? Yep sounds
familiar, except this time…

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:56.100
Not to mention that the apple of our hero's eye isn't a fair
maiden. She's a prostitute turned stripper.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.140
In this way, Deadpool can be seen as a spiritual
successor to a 400 ­year-­old novel that

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:07.910
also made fun of the heroes of its eras, employed
meta-narratives, and is called by some a proto­-postmodern

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:12.430
work: Don Quixote. Remember that the story
of Don Quixote is the tale of a fictional

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:17.060
author who discovered the fictional accounts
of Don Quixote, a story within a story, way

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:22.240
before The Blair Witch project. Don Quixote understands he is being written about,

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.640
and the fictional author
retelling his tale constantly breaks the fourth wall.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:32.419
At one point in Don Quixote, the story
just cuts off when the fictional author claims

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:36.569
the rest of the story was lost. We’re not
saying this parallel is intentional, but it is

00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:39.969
worth noting that Deadpool kills Don Quixote
in the comic books.

00:04:39.969 --> 00:04:44.189
To understand Don Quixote’s relation
to Deadpool, we have to understand its historical

00:04:44.189 --> 00:04:48.809
context. Don Quixote was written at a time
when radical skepticism of the existing order

00:04:48.809 --> 00:04:53.240
started to bud in popular society. It was
written just before the Enlightenment gripped Europe,

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.740
which would respond to this skepticism
by touting the virtues of rationality and science.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:01.440
But Don Quixote wasn’t setting
out to invent a new ideology: he just wanted

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.420
to have a few laughs at the expense of Spanish
society.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.939
Deadpool signals a similar disenchantment
with all those old righteous narratives in

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:12.620
our society. But this time it’s a little different, and whether or not it’s a good thing is up for debate.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.080
Part 2: Ironic Distance and Cynicism

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.820
In a recent interview, Joss Whedon, the man
behind such self­-aware titles as Firefly,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:27.110
Buffy and the Avengers, finds in Indiana Jones
a microcosm for all that’s wrong with today’s culture.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:31.300
In the original Indiana Jones film, our protagonist
famously deals with a gratuitous display of

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.559
swordsmanship with a modest display of marksmanship. In the Temple of Doom, we’re confronted

00:05:35.559 --> 00:05:40.279
with a similar scene but, oh my, there’s
now two swordsmen. Indie reaches for his gun,

00:05:40.279 --> 00:05:43.930
but it’s not there. He delivers a shit­eating-­grin
to the audience, as if to say, “see what

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:47.809
I did there? You saw that last movie, and
I made an inside joke about it. I’m so glad

00:05:47.809 --> 00:05:50.889
we had this moment together, now that you
feel like you’re in on this collective joke

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:54.060
that doesn’t even make sense because Temple
of Doom is a fucking prequel.”

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:58.780
That moment of self-­reference for the sake of self-­reference, Whedon says, has engulfed our culture.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:03.000
Film, television, literature ­it’s all becoming that
moment.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.880
This self­awareness is increasingly taking
a cynical tone. It’s almost everywhere you

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:16.694
look. In the new Tarzan trailer, a reboot/remake/adaptation of a film nobody asked for,

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:21.540
Jane overtly references the fact that she’s playing the tired role of a damsel in distress.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:30.029
Now what really chaps my ass about this is that the film is recognizing that it’s using tired recycled tropes, but

00:06:30.029 --> 00:06:34.419
instead of doing something new, it simply
apologizes for it, and expects us to perceive

00:06:34.419 --> 00:06:41.020
this self awareness as something clever and
new. The end of Star Trek Into Darkness is

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:47.100
just an excuse to say “Hey ­remember this
great scene in cinema? Now it’s opposite day!”

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.760
22 Jump Street is the same exact plot
as 21 Jump Street, but it’s ok, because

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:57.860
we can collectively identify with Ice Cube
for calling them out on their bullshit.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.180
Or, as South Park frames it:

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.960
As if to out­-meta each other, this cynical
attitude has transcended self­-reference

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:15.349
into self-­effacement. Hollywood, that decrepit
machine fueled by the ideas of yesteryear,

00:07:15.349 --> 00:07:24.339
is always stuck referencing itself. In an
effort reinvent itself while still remaining

00:07:24.339 --> 00:07:31.210
the same, it gleefully acknowledges its lazy
cliches, with a wink and a nod, like in Tarzan,

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.880
or by employing irony to undercut a dramatic
moment.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:39.960
Deadpool does both.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:44.339
It breaks the fourth wall to let us in on
the joke and it avoids serious drama like

00:07:44.339 --> 00:07:51.559
the plague. Hollywood still employs familiar
structures and tropes, but distances

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:55.620
the audience from any kind of sincerity so
that it feels new.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:04.840
Is this Hollywood version of #sorrynotsorry gratifying for the viewer,

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.610
or have we just come to terms with the fact

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.210
that there is nothing new to say?

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:12.999
Deadpool’s humor is largely driven by this
sense of cynicism and ironic distance. Whether

00:08:12.999 --> 00:08:18.990
he’s being tortured, or about to be gruesomely
maimed, Deadpool remains unphased in the face

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:23.360
of what would normally be considered high
stakes situations. Even the tension of combat

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:30.259
scenes are undercut by self­aware humor.
Also ­he can’t die so that probably helps.

00:08:30.259 --> 00:08:34.450
If Deadpool is immune to criticism, it’s
because he is defined by his cynicism. He

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:42.099
relentlessly deconstructs any trope that would
endow meaning to film. Deadpool is more or

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:48.790
less Wade Wilson’s journey to become hot
again, itself a shallow endeavor, even if

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:57.139
to reclaim his lost love­ or really lust. Deadpool
evades criticism become he has no moral scruples

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:00.000
or values to defend,

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.300
his identity is purely
reactionary.

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:12.000
He risks nothing. He just makes fun of everything.
Deadpool doesn’t claim to be a good guy.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.430
The film also has 0 fucks to give. Its ad
campaign doesn’t take itself seriously and

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:21.300
the movie points out its glaring flaws: The
overuse of superhero tropes,

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.440
or the obvious budgetary constraints.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.860
I mean, Negasonic Teenage Warhead
is the Marvel Universe equivalent of a Walmart

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:43.320
bargain bin DVD. But just because the movie
is aware of, and sort of apologizes for, it’s lacking qualities,

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:44.880
does that make it good?

00:09:44.889 --> 00:09:49.130
I have to admit, I’m conflicted here. On
the one hand, I find Deadpool’s self­reflective

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:53.560
edgy commentary to be undeniably enjoyable.
At the end of the day, Ryan Reynolds is damn

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:58.209
charming, the action is awesome, and the jokes
are disarming and quite fun. But then again,

00:09:58.209 --> 00:10:02.250
should we really give so much praise to a
movie that essentially functions like an Honest

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:04.470
Trailer for other comic book movies?

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:08.690
Therein lies a danger with this trend: it
never creates anything. An obsession

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.140
with being meta that devolves into an endless
spiral of meta.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:19.080
In this way, Deadpool isn’t

00:10:19.089 --> 00:10:24.129
entirely dissimilar from another cultural
movement: one that ironically loves the cliche,

00:10:24.129 --> 00:10:29.550
endlessly recycles culture and constantly
positions itself against the mainstream­­-hipsters.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:34.629
As Douglas Haddow once aptly noted, the danger
of the hipster is the end of Western civilization

00:10:34.629 --> 00:10:40.829
itself, “a culture lost in the superficiality
of its past and unable to create any new meaning.”

00:10:40.829 --> 00:10:45.000
Deadpool isn’t reinventing the superhero
genre, it can only exist to make fun of the genre.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.980
Here’s the real problem with Deadpool
and other films like it. Bashing the system

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:58.779
may feel good, it may make us laugh, but it’s
ultimately powerless­ it doesn’t change

00:10:58.779 --> 00:11:00.089
the system at all.

00:11:00.089 --> 00:11:03.949
As our old friend and alleged Mark Hamill
body­double Slavoj Zizek notes, “cynical

00:11:03.949 --> 00:11:09.259
distance, laughter, irony, are, so to speak,
part of the game. The ruling ideology is not

00:11:09.259 --> 00:11:11.600
meant to be taken seriously or literally.”

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:12.720
Hipsters fancy themselves

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:17.540
members of the counterculture by drinking
PBR and having ironic mustaches. Deadpool

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:22.009
fancies itself a countercultural movie by
celebrating its own played-­out cliches and

00:11:22.009 --> 00:11:28.500
an overused storyline, the cinematic equivalents of gross hipster mustaches and non-­prescription glasses.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:33.190
This cultural impasse reflects a society that
doesn’t quite know what to do with itself.

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:38.160
We beat the Commies, our music, film and literature
dominate the world market, and as a society

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.810
we’re not exactly on the brink of collapse
­ despite what political pundits are telling you.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:46.620
If Deadpool can say “Fuck it,” it’s
because he doesn’t fear death. How can he

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:51.089
take anything seriously when there are no consequences? Can life be sacred when it has become

00:11:51.089 --> 00:11:58.029
limitless? Hell, if the superhero genre survived
these dumpster fires, then maybe it can’t

00:11:58.029 --> 00:12:01.760
die either­ so fuck it. Why not break all
the rules?

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.930
Interestingly, the very sanctity-­breaking
of the comic book was inspired a similar problem.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:11.420
Except, rather than feeling that Deadpool
the comic was uncancellable, they felt it

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:16.230
would be cancelled at any minute. What ensued
was a similar attitude of “fuck it,” if

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:19.080
the comic was already dead, why not write
it like nothing mattered?

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:24.560
If we, as a society, can say “fuck it,” perhaps it’s because death or really any real existential threat

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:25.760
has become either so

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.560
distance, or so close that we no longer care.

00:12:28.569 --> 00:12:32.899
So where does Deadpool land in our cultural
landscape? Does it just use the fourth wall

00:12:32.899 --> 00:12:37.529
breaking to put a shiny makeover over the
same old shit? Is it the ultimate cynical

00:12:37.529 --> 00:12:43.720
film? Or does it actually achieve something
new? Let us know what you think in the comments.

